==English==

===Etymology===
Assibilated variant of {{m|en|hunk}}, of uncertain origin.

Alternatively, a derivative of {{m|en|hump}}, via an earlier Middle English {{m|enm|*hunche}}, {{m|enm|*humpchin}}, from {{suffix|enm||alt1=*hump|-chen|alt2=-chin, -chen|pos2=diminutive suffix|nocat=1}}, equivalent to {{af|en|hump|-kin|nocat=1}}. In the sense of an intuitive impression, said to be from the old gambling superstition that it brings luck to touch the hump of a hunchback.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hʌnt͡ʃ/|/hʌnʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-hunch.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌntʃ|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[hump]]; a [[protuberance]].
# A [[stoop]]ed or [[curl]]ed [[posture]]; a [[slouch]].
#: {{ux|en|The old man walked with a '''hunch'''.}}
# A [[theory]], [[idea]], or [[guess]]; an [[intuitive]] [[impression]] that something will happen.
#: {{ux|en|I have a '''hunch''' they'll find a way to solve the problem.}}
# A [[hunk]]; a lump; a thick piece.
#: {{ux|en|a '''hunch''' of bread}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage="The greedy little bastards! They had a ''''unch''' of bread each when I 'ad my dinner."}}
# A push or thrust, as with the elbow.

