===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To bend the top of one's body forward while [[raise|raising]] one's shoulders.
#: {{syn|en|slouch|stoop|lean}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't '''hunch''' over your computer if you want to avoid neck problems.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author={{w|Muriel Spark}}|title={{w|The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie}}|location=New York|publisher=HarperPerenniel|year_published=1994|chapter=5|page=156|url=https://archive.org/details/primeofmissjeanb00muri_1
|passage=Sandy, you will never get anywhere by '''hunching''' over your [[putter]], hold your shoulders back and bend from the waist.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author={{w|Armistead Maupin}}|title={{w|Tales of the City}}|location=New York|publisher=Ballantine Books|chapter=… and many happy returns|page=76|url=https://archive.org/details/talesofcity00armi
|passage=She rolled over and '''hunched''' into a [[fetal position]].}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To raise (one's shoulders) (while lowering one's head or bending the top of one's body forward); to curve (one's body) forward (''sometimes followed by'' [[up]]).
#: {{ux|en|They stood outside the door '''hunching''' themselves against the rain and puffing on their cigarettes.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''hunched''' up his shoulders and stared down at the ground.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1672|author={{w|Edward Ravenscroft}}|title=The Citizen Turn’d Gentleman|location=London|publisher=Thomas Dring|section=act I, scene 1|page=4|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A51115.0001.001
|passage=''Danc[ing] Mast[er].'' {{...}} not too fast {{...}} keep you[r] leg[s] straight, {{...}} don’t '''hunch''' up your shoulders so;}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1924|author={{w|Ford Madox Ford}}|title={{w|Some Do Not ...}}|location=New York|publisher=Grosset & Dunlap|section=Part 2, Chapter 2|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20140712/html.php
|passage=If you '''hunch''' your shoulders too long against a storm your shoulders will grow bowed....}}
#* '''1938''', {{w|Marjorie Kinnan Rawlings}}, New York: Scribner, Chapter{{nbsp}}17,<sup>[https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20171116/html.php]</sup>
#*: He would '''hunch''' his twisted body close and put out his gentle and crooked hand and touch the fawn.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1939|author={{w|John Steinbeck}}|title={{w|The Grapes of Wrath}}|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.215897|chapter=10|page=142|publisher=Viking|year_published=1958|location=New York
|passage=They sat looking out at the dark, at the square of light the kitchen lantern threw on the ground outside the door, with a '''hunched''' shadow of Grampa in the middle of it.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[walk]] (somewhere) while hunching one's shoulders.
#: {{syn|en|slouch}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author={{w|J. P. Donleavy}}|title={{w|The Ginger Man}}|location=New York|publisher=Dell|chapter=2|page=9|url=https://archive.org/details/gingerman00donl
|passage={{...}} the figure '''hunched''' up the road.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1969|author={{w|Ray Bradbury}}|chapter=The Inspired Chicken Motel|title={{w|I Sing the Body Electric (short story collection)|I Sing the Body Electric}}|url=https://archive.org/details/isingbodyelectri00brad|page=57|publisher=Knopf|location=New York
|passage={{...}} once we had '''hunched''' in out of the sun and slunk through a cold pork-and-beans-on-bread lunch {{...}} my brother and I found a desert creek nearby and heaved rocks at each other to cool off.}}
#* '''1983''', {{w|Jack Vance}}, ''{{w|Suldrun's Garden|Suldrun’s Garden}},'' Spatterlight Press, 2012, Chapter{{nbsp}}18,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=1nKJwiXe_uEC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: {{...}} wheezing and grunting he '''hunched''' across the room.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[thrust]] a [[hump]] or [[protuberance]] out of (something); to [[crook]], as the back.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1679|author={{w|John Dryden}}|author2={{w|Nathaniel Lee}}|title={{w|Oedipus (Dryden play)|Oedipus}}|location=London|publisher=R. Bentley and M. Magnes|section=act I|page=6|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A36657.0001.001
|passage={{...}} thou art all one errour; soul and body.<br>The first young tryal of some unskill’d Pow’r;<br>Rude in the making Art, and Ape of Jove.<br>Thy crooked mind within '''hunch’d''' out thy back;<br>And wander’d in thy limbs:}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[push]] or [[jostle]] with the [[elbow]]; to push or thrust against (someone).
#: {{syn|en|elbow|nudge}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1667|translator={{w|Roger L'Estrange|Roger L’Estrange}}|title={{w|Los Sueños|The Visions}} of {{w|Francisco de Quevedo|Dom Francisco de Quevedo Villegas}}|location=London|publisher=H. Herringman|chapter=The Sixth Vision of Hell|pages=182–183|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A56893.0001.001
|passage=After this, we saw a great Troop of ''Women'' upon the ''High-way to Hell,'' with their ''Bags;'' and their ''fellows,'' at their Heels, ever, and anon, '''hunching''', and Justling one Another.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1748|author={{w|Samuel Richardson}}|title={{w|Clarissa}}|location=London|section=Volume 2, Letter 1, p. 8|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004835420.0001.002
|passage=Hickman, a great over-grown, lank-hair’d, chubby boy, who would be '''hunch’d''' and punch’d by every-body; and go home, with his finger in his eye, and tell his mother.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1899|author={{w|Sutton E. Griggs}}|title=Imperium in Imperio|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15454/pg15454-images.html|chapter=6
|passage=He let his eyes scan the faces of all the white teachers, male and female, but would end up with a stare at the colored man sitting there. Finally, he '''hunched''' his seat-mate with his elbow and asked what man that was.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author={{w|Maya Angelou}}|title={{w|Gather Together in My Name}}|url=https://archive.org/details/gathertogetherinangerich|chapter=12|page=40|publisher=Bantam|year_published=1975|location=New York
|passage=She '''hunched''' me and winked.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author={{w|Billy Roche}}|title=Tumbling Down|location=Dublin|publisher=Wolfhound Press|year_published=1994|chapter=6|pages=102–103|url=https://archive.org/details/tumblingdown00roch
|passage={{...}} Crunch burst through, pretending to be in {{w|Croke Park}} or somewhere, '''hunching''' me away with his shoulder and holding the ghost of other players at bay as he picked up the football.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To have a hunch, or make an intuitive guess.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Max Gunther|title=The Luck Factor|passage=People who are instinctive hunchers go through some such process at every decision-making point of their lives. It is likely that children often make decisions and discern truths by '''hunching'''.}}

